window.EXAMDATA=window.EXAMDATA||{lessons:[],qs:[],terms:[]};
EXAMDATA.lessons.push(
 {id:'FL-4.1.1',ch:4,k:'K2',title:'Black-box, white-box and experience-based techniques',
  plain:'Test techniques help you choose good test cases. There are three families. Black-box techniques work from what the software should DO. White-box techniques work from how the code is BUILT. Experience-based techniques lean on the skill and knowledge of the people testing.',
  coffee:'Black-box is judging a latte by tasting it against the menu description. White-box is inspecting the espresso machine and its pipes. Experience-based is the head barista saying, "I know this grinder always jams on the dark roast, check that."',
  points:['Black-box (specification-based): test cases come from the specified behaviour of the test object, not from its internal structure, so they stay useful if the implementation changes but the behaviour does not. Examples: equivalence partitioning, boundary value analysis, decision table testing, state transition testing.','White-box (structure-based): test cases come from the internal structure and processing of the test object, so they can only be made once the design or code exists. Examples: statement testing and branch testing, measured as coverage.','Experience-based: test cases come from the knowledge and experience of the testers, so effectiveness depends heavily on their skill. Examples: error guessing, exploratory testing, checklist-based testing.','Black-box finds gaps between behaviour and specification; white-box measures how much of the code was exercised; experience-based can find defects that black-box and white-box techniques miss.','The families complement each other, so they are often combined.'],
  trap:'Do not call error guessing or exploratory testing "black-box" just because the tester does not read code; the syllabus puts them in their own experience-based family.'},
 {id:'FL-4.2.1',ch:4,k:'K3',title:'Equivalence partitioning (derive test cases)',
  plain:'Split the possible inputs into groups (partitions) where the software should treat every value the same way. Then test just one value from each group. Valid partitions hold values that should be accepted; invalid partitions hold values that should be rejected.',
  coffee:'A shop sells 1 to 12 cups per order. You do not need to test every number: one value from "1 to 12" (valid), one from "0 or less" (invalid) and one from "13 or more" (invalid) tell you most of what you need.',
  points:['Step 1: list the inputs and the rule for each (e.g. cups per order must be whole numbers 1 to 12).','Step 2: divide the range into partitions: invalid low (0 and below), valid (1 to 12), invalid high (13 and above). Every possible value belongs to exactly one partition.','Step 3: write test cases so that every partition, valid and invalid, is exercised at least once. A good habit is to keep each invalid value in its own test so one rejection does not hide another.','Worked example: tests with 6 cups (valid), 0 cups (invalid low) and 20 cups (invalid high) give 100% valid and invalid partition coverage.','Coverage = partitions exercised by at least one test / total identified partitions. Partitions must not overlap and must not be empty; they can be made for outputs, time values or non-numeric sets such as card type.','With several inputs, Each Choice coverage is the simplest criterion: every partition of every input appears in at least one test. It ignores combinations of partitions.'],
  trap:'Picking several values from the same partition adds no coverage; and do not forget the invalid partitions.'},
 {id:'FL-4.2.2',ch:4,k:'K3',title:'Boundary value analysis (derive test cases)',
  plain:'Bugs love edges. Boundary value analysis (BVA) takes the ordered partitions from equivalence partitioning and tests the values at the edges of each partition. It works for ordered partitions, such as numbers or dates.',
  coffee:'If a loyalty discount starts at 10 cups, the cashier is most likely to slip at 9, 10 and 11, not at 3 or 7.',
  points:['Step 1: find the ordered partitions (equivalence partitioning) and mark the minimum and maximum value of each; these are the boundary values. BVA cannot be used on unordered partitions.','2-value BVA: for each boundary value, test it and its closest neighbour that belongs to the ADJACENT partition. Valid range 1 to 12 gives tests 0, 1, 12, 13 (4 tests).','3-value BVA: for each boundary value, test it and BOTH its neighbours (one on each side). Range 1 to 12 gives 0, 1, 2, 11, 12, 13 (6 tests).','Step 2: write expected results: 0 and 13 rejected, 1, 2, 11 and 12 accepted.','Coverage = coverage items exercised / coverage items identified (in 2-value BVA these are the boundary values; in 3-value BVA also their neighbours). 3-value BVA is stronger: it can catch defects the 2-value version misses, e.g. a condition "x <= 10" wrongly coded as "x = 10" is only exposed by x = 9.','Worked example: discount for 10 or more cups. Looking only at the edge between the partitions 1-9 and 10+: boundary values 9 and 10. 2-value tests: 9 and 10. 3-value tests: 8, 9, 10 and 11.'],
  trap:'In 2-value BVA the second value of each pair sits in the other partition, not inside the same one.'},
 {id:'FL-4.2.3',ch:4,k:'K3',title:'Decision table testing (derive test cases)',
  plain:'Use a decision table when the outcome depends on a combination of conditions. List every condition and action, then each column is one rule (one combination) and one test case. It makes sure you do not forget combinations.',
  coffee:'A free-pastry rule: loyalty member AND bought a large coffee. The four combinations of yes/no on those two conditions each give a column, with the action "give free pastry" ticked only in one.',
  points:['Step 1: list the conditions (inputs) and the actions (outputs) from the test basis.','Step 2: build the full table: with n yes/no conditions there are 2 to the power n combinations (columns). Two conditions give 4 columns.','Step 3: fill in the expected actions for each column from the rules.','Step 4: simplify by deleting columns with infeasible combinations (marked N/A) and, if wanted, minimise by merging columns where a condition is irrelevant (marked "-").','Step 5: each remaining column becomes at least one test case; coverage = columns exercised / total feasible columns.','Worked example: member (Y/N) and large coffee (Y/N): only Y,Y gives a free pastry. Tests: Y,Y -> pastry; Y,N -> none; N,Y -> none; N,N -> none.','Limited-entry tables use only true/false (T/F, or Y/N) values; extended-entry tables allow other values such as ranges or partitions. The strength: no combination is overlooked, and gaps or contradictions in the rules show up.'],
  trap:'Counting columns wrongly: a full table with 3 yes/no conditions has 8 columns, not 6; only merge columns when the rules really make a condition irrelevant.'},
 {id:'FL-4.2.4',ch:4,k:'K3',title:'State transition testing (derive test cases)',
  plain:'Some software behaves differently depending on what has already happened. A state transition diagram shows states, events that cause moves, and the actions that result. You derive tests that walk through the states and transitions.',
  coffee:'An order goes Placed -> Paid -> Brewing -> Ready -> Collected. Trying to collect it while it is still Placed should be refused: that is an invalid transition worth testing.',
  points:['Step 1: read the model (a state diagram or equivalent state table): states (boxes), transitions (arrows), events (triggers), guard conditions and actions.','Step 2: derive tests. Coverage can be all states, valid transitions, or sequences of transitions.','All-states coverage: every state is visited at least once. Valid-transitions (0-switch) coverage: every single valid transition is taken at least once. All-transitions coverage exercises all valid transitions AND attempts the invalid ones (the empty cells of a state table), one invalid transition per test to avoid defect masking.','Worked example: for Placed -> Paid -> Ready, a test "pay, then mark ready" covers 3 states and 2 transitions. Add "mark ready while still Placed" to check the invalid transition is refused.','Relationships: all states is the weakest; full valid transitions coverage guarantees all states coverage; full all-transitions coverage guarantees both. Valid transitions coverage is the most widely used.','A test case is usually a sequence of events giving a sequence of state changes (and actions); one test normally covers several transitions. Transition label syntax: event [guard] / action.'],
  trap:'A state is not the same as an event: arrows are transitions triggered by events, boxes are states.'},
 {id:'FL-4.3.1',ch:4,k:'K2',title:'Statement testing and statement coverage',
  plain:'Statement testing designs tests so that executable statements in the code are run. Statement coverage is the number of statements your tests ran divided by the total number of executable statements, as a percentage.',
  coffee:'Like a cleaner who must at least walk through every room of the shop once, whether or not they check every corner of each room.',
  points:['Statement coverage = executed statements / total executable statements x 100%.','100% statement coverage means every executable statement ran at least once.','It does not mean every decision outcome was taken: an if without an else can reach 100% statement coverage with only the "true" test.','Tests are designed from the code, so it is a white-box technique.','Example: code has 5 statements, tests run 4 of them: 80% statement coverage.'],
  trap:'100% statement coverage is not "everything is tested"; false outcomes of decisions can still be untested.'},
 {id:'FL-4.3.2',ch:4,k:'K2',title:'Branch testing and branch coverage',
  plain:'Branch testing designs tests so that the branches in the code are taken. A branch is a transfer of control, for example the "true" and "false" outcome of an if. Branch coverage is branches exercised divided by total branches.',
  coffee:'At every junction in the shop (queue or table service?) you send a customer down BOTH paths at least once, not just the busy one.',
  points:['Branch coverage = branches exercised / total branches x 100%. A simple if has a true and a false outcome, even if there is no else.','Branch coverage is stronger than statement coverage.','100% branch coverage guarantees 100% statement coverage, but 100% statement coverage does not guarantee 100% branch coverage.','It covers unconditional branches (straight-line flow) and conditional ones (if outcomes, case outcomes, loop exit or continue). Exam questions usually count decision outcomes.','Example: two separate ifs have 4 decision outcomes (when counting only those); tests that only take both "true" outcomes reach 50%.'],
  trap:'The direction is one way only: branch implies statement, not the reverse.'},
 {id:'FL-4.3.3',ch:4,k:'K2',title:'The value of white-box testing',
  plain:'White-box techniques look at the actual implementation, so they can find problems even when the requirements are vague, missing or out of date. They also give an objective measure of how much of the code your tests touched.',
  coffee:'Checking the espresso machine itself shows broken parts even if the recipe book is half missing.',
  points:['The whole software implementation is taken into account, which helps find defects even when the specification is vague or outdated.','Coverage gives an objective measurement of how thoroughly the structure was tested.','White-box techniques can also be used in static testing, for example dry runs of code or reviewing code and pseudocode that is not yet ready to execute.','A weakness: if the software does not implement a requirement at all (a defect of omission), white-box tests based on the code may not reveal it.','They are normally combined with black-box and experience-based techniques.'],
  trap:'White-box testing is not limited to running code; it also applies to static work such as code reviews.'},
 {id:'FL-4.4.1',ch:4,k:'K2',title:'Error guessing',
  plain:'Error guessing means anticipating where mistakes and defects are likely, using your experience, and then designing tests to expose them. You guess errors, defects and failures, and write tests aimed at each.',
  coffee:'A barista who has seen the card machine hang before will try paying with a cancelled card and an empty basket just to see.',
  points:['Based on knowledge of how the application has worked in the past, the errors developers tend to make, and failures seen in similar applications.','Typical areas for guessed errors: input (correct input not accepted), output (wrong format), logic (wrong operator), computation, interfaces and data (wrong initialisation).','A structured way is to build a list of defect and failure types, and design tests that attack each (a "fault attack").','Sources for the list: experience, defect data, and general knowledge about why software fails.','It is an experience-based technique and does not rely on a formal model.'],
  trap:'Error guessing is not random clicking; it is targeted at specific, plausible errors.'},
 {id:'FL-4.4.2',ch:4,k:'K2',title:'Exploratory testing',
  plain:'In exploratory testing the tester designs, runs and learns from tests at the same time, using what each test reveals to choose the next one. It is often organised as time-boxed sessions guided by a short test charter.',
  coffee:'Like tasting a new blend: each sip tells you what to try next, within a set 30 minutes and with a goal like "judge the aftertaste".',
  points:['Tests are designed, executed and evaluated at the same time, with the tester learning about the software as they go.','Session-based testing: a test session is time-boxed and guided by a test charter containing the test objectives.','Afterwards there is a debriefing, a discussion between the tester and stakeholders interested in the results; session sheets may record the steps and discoveries.','Most useful when specifications are poor or missing, or when there is severe time pressure.','It complements other, more formal, test techniques and can itself use them (e.g. equivalence partitioning). It works best with an experienced tester who has domain knowledge, curiosity and analytical skill.'],
  trap:'Exploratory does not mean unplanned: session-based testing still has a charter, a timebox and a debrief.'},
 {id:'FL-4.4.3',ch:4,k:'K2',title:'Checklist-based testing',
  plain:'In checklist-based testing the tester designs, implements and runs tests to cover items on a checklist. The checklist is built from experience, from knowledge of what matters to users, and from understanding why and how software fails.',
  coffee:'The shop opening checklist: machine warmed, till float counted, milk fresh. Each item is checked every day, but how you check it can vary.',
  points:['Checklists can be built for functional, non-functional or other areas (e.g. usability).','Items are often phrased as questions and should each be checkable separately and directly; avoid items a tool can check, items that belong in entry or exit criteria, and items that are too general.','If the checklist is high level, the actual testing varies between testers, giving potentially greater coverage but less repeatability.','Checklists should not be static: update them from defect analysis, add items for newly found severe defects, drop entries that stopped being effective, and keep the list from growing too long.','In the absence of detailed test cases, a checklist gives guidelines and some consistency.'],
  trap:'A checklist item is not a step-by-step test case with exact data and results.'},
 {id:'FL-4.5.1',ch:4,k:'K2',title:'Writing user stories in collaboration',
  plain:'A user story describes a feature from the point of view of a user or customer. Testers, developers and business people write and refine stories together so everyone understands them and can test them.',
  coffee:'"As a regular customer I want to reorder my usual drink so I skip the queue." The barista, owner and customer representative agree what "reorder" means before anything is built.',
  points:['A user story represents a feature valuable to a user or purchaser. Its three critical aspects are the 3 Cs: Card (the medium describing the story), Conversation (explains how the software will be used) and Confirmation (the acceptance criteria).','Good stories follow INVEST: Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable.','Collaborative writing (e.g. brainstorming or mind mapping) brings three perspectives together: business, development and testing, giving a shared vision of what will be delivered.','Common format: "As a [role], I want [goal], so that [business value]", followed by the acceptance criteria.','Testers help by asking questions, spotting ambiguity and untestable statements, and proposing acceptance criteria.','If a stakeholder does not know how to test a story, it may be unclear, may not reflect something valuable to them, or they may just need help with testing.'],
  trap:'Confirmation in the 3 Cs refers to the acceptance criteria, not to a manager approving the story.'},
 {id:'FL-4.5.2',ch:4,k:'K2',title:'Options for writing acceptance criteria',
  plain:'Acceptance criteria are the conditions a story must meet to be accepted. They can be written in a few formats: scenario-oriented (Given/When/Then), rule-oriented (a list of rules or a table), or other formats the team agrees on.',
  coffee:'Scenario: "Given an unpaid order, when the barista taps Ready, then the till says payment needed". Rule list: "Orders over R200 deliver free."',
  points:['Purposes: define the scope of the story, reach consensus among stakeholders, describe positive and negative scenarios, serve as the basis for acceptance testing, and allow accurate planning and estimation.','Scenario-oriented: Given (precondition), When (action or event), Then (expected result), often used with behaviour-driven development.','Rule-oriented: a bullet list of rules to verify, or a table that maps inputs to outputs.','Other formats are allowed if criteria are well defined and unambiguous.','Criteria should be testable and agreed with the whole team.'],
  trap:'A table of inputs and expected outputs is rule-oriented, not scenario-oriented.'},
 {id:'FL-4.5.3',ch:4,k:'K3',title:'Acceptance test-driven development (ATDD)',
  plain:'ATDD is a test-first approach. Before coding, the team (business, developers and testers) turns a user story and its acceptance criteria into acceptance tests. Developers then build until those tests pass.',
  coffee:'Agree the taste test for a new drink BEFORE the barista invents the recipe, then keep adjusting the recipe until it passes.',
  points:['Test cases are created before the story is implemented. The first step is a specification workshop where the story and its criteria are analysed and written by team members with different perspectives (customer, developer, tester).','Step 1: take the user story and its acceptance criteria and find gaps or ambiguities.','Step 2: write the first test as a positive test (the main successful path) without variations.','Step 3: add negative tests, then cover non-functional characteristics such as performance efficiency or usability; test techniques from 4.2 to 4.4 may be used.','Tests are written in the language of the business domain so all stakeholders understand them; they can be written as examples and sometimes automated.','The tests must cover all characteristics of the story and must not go beyond it; no two tests should describe the same characteristic. Tests state preconditions, inputs and expected results (postconditions). Order: positive tests first, then negative, then non-functional.'],
  trap:'The tests are written BEFORE the code, and they must follow the story and its criteria, not invent new rules.'}
);
EXAMDATA.qs.push(
 {id:'c4-001',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.1.1',k:'K2',q:'A tester prepares tests purely from the written requirements, without ever opening the source code. Which statement best explains why this is a black-box approach?',o:['The tests are run by a tester who is independent of the developer who wrote the code.','The test cases are derived from specified behaviour, not from how the code is built.','The tester relies on personal knowledge of earlier failures to decide what to check.','The tests are chosen so that a measured level of code coverage is reached.'],a:1,why:'Correct: black-box techniques derive tests from the specified behaviour of the test object, not from its internal structure. Wrong: independence of the person running the tests is a different concept; relying on knowledge of earlier failures describes experience-based techniques; targeting a coverage level describes white-box techniques.'},
 {id:'c4-002',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.1.1',k:'K2',q:'A developer writes extra tests until every branch in a module has been taken at least once and reports the coverage figure. Which family of techniques is this?',o:['Black-box, because the tests check the module outputs against expected results.','Experience-based, because the developer already knows the module well.','Black-box, because the module has written requirements behind it.','White-box, because tests and coverage are driven by the code structure.'],a:3,why:'Correct: designing tests for structural coverage such as branches is white-box. Wrong: checking outputs happens in every family; knowing the module does not make the technique experience-based; having requirements does not make a structure-driven technique black-box.'},
 {id:'c4-003',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.1.1',k:'K2',q:'Which choice lists techniques that all belong to the SAME family?',o:['Statement testing, equivalence partitioning, error guessing','Branch testing, decision table testing, exploratory testing','Error guessing, exploratory testing, checklist-based testing','Boundary value analysis, branch testing, checklist-based testing'],a:2,why:'Correct: error guessing, exploratory testing and checklist-based testing are all experience-based. Wrong: statement testing + equivalence partitioning + error guessing mixes white-box, black-box and experience-based; branch testing + decision table testing + exploratory testing mixes white-box, black-box and experience-based; boundary value analysis + branch testing + checklist-based testing mixes black-box, white-box and experience-based.'},
 {id:'c4-004',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.1.1',k:'K2',q:'The specification for a new till feature is thin and the release is in two days. Why might the team add experience-based techniques to its testing?',o:['They replace the need for a test basis entirely, so a thin specification no longer matters to the team or the schedule.','They guarantee full code coverage, which a thin specification could never provide for the till software on its own.','They use tester knowledge to find defects systematic techniques may miss, with little documentation.','Black-box techniques cannot be applied to a till at all, so only experience-based ones are left for the team.'],a:2,why:'Correct: experience-based techniques complement systematic ones and work with little documentation. Wrong: they do not remove the need for a test basis; they give no code coverage guarantee; black-box techniques can be used on any software with a specified behaviour.'},
 {id:'c4-005',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.1.1',k:'K2',q:'Match each technique to what its test cases are mainly based on. Which pairing is correct?',o:['Statement testing - the requirements document and its acceptance criteria','Exploratory testing - a code coverage target set for the module','Equivalence partitioning - the specified input and output behaviour','Boundary value analysis - the tester\'s memory of defects in earlier releases'],a:2,why:'Correct: equivalence partitioning works from specified behaviour. Wrong: statement testing is based on the code; exploratory testing is based on tester knowledge and learning, not a coverage target; boundary value analysis is based on ordered partitions of specified behaviour, not memories of defects.'},
 {id:'c4-006',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.1',k:'K2',q:'Which statement best explains what statement coverage measures?',o:['The share of executable statements in the code that the tests ran at least once.','The share of requirements that have at least one test written against them.','The share of decision outcomes in the code that the tests have taken.','The share of defects found compared with the number of statements written.'],a:0,why:'Correct: statement coverage is executed statements over total executable statements. Wrong: requirements coverage is a different measure; decision outcomes relate to branch coverage; defect density is not a coverage measure.'},
 {id:'c4-007',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.1',k:'K2',q:'Code: (1) read t; (2) if t < 0 then; (3) t = 0; (4) end if; (5) print t. Executable statements are read, if, assignment and print (4 in total). A single test with t = -5 is run. What is the statement coverage?',o:['75%','50%','25%','100%'],a:3,why:'Correct: t = -5 makes the condition true, so read, if, the assignment and print all run: 4 of 4 = 100%. Wrong: 75%, 50% and 25% assume some statements were skipped, but none were. (The untaken false outcome of the if does not reduce statement coverage.)'},
 {id:'c4-008',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.1',k:'K2',q:'Code: read qty; if qty >= 10 then price = 8 else price = 10; print qty*price. There are 5 executable statements (read, if, two assignments, print). One test uses qty = 12. What is the statement coverage?',o:['60%','80%','100%','40%'],a:1,why:'Correct: the test runs read, if, price = 8 and print, which is 4 of 5 = 80%. The else assignment is never run. Wrong: 60% and 40% undercount; 100% would need a second test through the else path.'},
 {id:'c4-009',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.1',k:'K2',q:'A report says a test suite achieved 100% statement coverage. What can be concluded?',o:['Every decision outcome in the code was taken at least once, including every false outcome.','Every statement ran at least once, but some decision outcomes may be untaken.','The statements that ran contain no defects, because each was exercised by a test.','The requirements are fully covered, because every statement of the code was executed.'],a:1,why:'Correct: statement coverage only tells you the statements ran; an if without an else can still have an untaken false outcome. Wrong: taking every decision outcome is branch coverage; running a statement does not prove it is defect free (for example data-dependent failures); the measure says nothing about requirement coverage.'},
 {id:'c4-010',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.2',k:'K2',q:'Which statement best explains branch coverage?',o:['The number of statements run divided by the number of lines of code in the module.','The share of requirements verified by at least one test that was executed.','The share of branches, such as the true and false outcomes of decisions, that the tests exercised.','The share of modules that were integrated and tested together so far in the project.'],a:2,why:'Correct: branch coverage counts exercised branches over all branches. Wrong: dividing statements by lines is neither branch coverage nor a statement-coverage measure (lines are not the same as executable statements); requirements coverage and integration progress are not code-branch measures.'},
 {id:'c4-011',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.2',k:'K2',q:'Code: read a; if a > 0 then x = 1; if a > 5 then y = 2; print. The two ifs are separate and have no else. Count only the true and false outcomes of the two decisions as branches (4 in total). Test a = 10 and test a = 3 are run. What is the branch coverage?',o:['100%','50%','75%','25%'],a:2,why:'Correct: a = 10 takes true for both ifs; a = 3 takes true for the first and false for the second. Covered: if1-true, if2-true and if2-false = 3 of 4 = 75%. The false outcome of the first if is never taken. Wrong: 100% needs a test with a <= 0; 50% and 25% undercount.'},
 {id:'c4-012',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.2',k:'K2',q:'Which statement about the relationship between branch coverage and statement coverage is correct?',o:['100% statement coverage guarantees 100% branch coverage of the same code.','100% branch coverage guarantees 100% statement coverage, but not the other way round.','The two are always equal for the same test suite, whatever the code looks like.','Neither percentage implies anything about the other, so both must always be measured.'],a:1,why:'Correct: covering all branches means every path out of every decision is taken, so every statement runs; the reverse fails for an if without an else (branch coverage subsumes statement coverage). Wrong: claiming statement coverage guarantees branch coverage reverses the relationship; the two are not always equal; and they are related, not independent.'},
 {id:'c4-013',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.2',k:'K2',q:'Code: read t; if t < 0 then t = 0; print t. What is the smallest number of tests needed for 100% branch coverage, and why?',o:['1, because a single test already reaches every statement in this short piece of code.','2, because the if must be taken as both true and false, even without an else.','3, because the code has three statements and each statement needs a test of its own.','4, because every statement needs both a true test and a false test to be fully checked.'],a:1,why:'Correct: the if has a true and a false outcome, so two tests are needed. Wrong: one test can give statement coverage but not branch coverage; the number of statements does not drive the branch count; true and false outcomes belong to decisions, not to every statement.'},
 {id:'c4-014',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.3',k:'K2',q:'Which statement best explains one value of white-box testing?',o:['It can find defects even when the specification is vague or out of date, because the whole implementation is considered.','It reliably finds requirements that were never implemented.','It removes the need for black-box testing.','It can only be applied once the system is fully running.'],a:0,why:'Correct: using the implementation lets you test regardless of specification quality. Wrong: unimplemented requirements are a known weakness of white-box testing; it complements black-box testing rather than replacing it; white-box thinking also applies in static testing such as dry runs of code and code review.'},
 {id:'c4-015',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.3',k:'K2',q:'A team achieves 100% branch coverage on the order module. Which problem is white-box testing alone least likely to reveal?',o:['A statement that was never executed by any test.','A decision that always takes the same outcome in every test.','A branch that no test has ever taken.','A requirement that was never implemented in the code at all.'],a:3,why:'Correct: tests derived from the code cannot show something that is absent from the code. Wrong: unexecuted statements and untaken branches are exactly what coverage measurement exposes, and a decision with only one outcome would show up as incomplete branch coverage.'},
 {id:'c4-016',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.3.3',k:'K2',q:'Which is an advantage that coverage measurement brings to white-box testing?',o:['It proves the software is free of defects as soon as the agreed coverage target has been reached.','It gives an objective measure of how much of the code structure the tests exercised.','It guarantees that all user needs are met by the finished software once coverage is high.','It replaces reviews and static analysis of the code, so those activities can be dropped.'],a:1,why:'Correct: coverage is an objective measure of structural thoroughness and shows where extra tests are needed. Wrong: no technique proves the absence of defects; coverage says nothing about user needs; it does not replace static testing, which white-box ideas can in fact support.'},
 {id:'c4-017',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.1',k:'K2',q:'Which statement best explains error guessing?',o:['Deriving tests from partitions of the input range, then picking one representative value from each partition.','Choosing tests at random without any reasoning behind them, in the hope that some will hit a defect.','Measuring which statements have been executed so far and reporting the result as a percentage.','Anticipating likely errors from past defects and typical mistakes, then designing tests to expose them.'],a:3,why:'Correct: error guessing relies on experience of likely errors, defects and failures. Wrong: partitioning is equivalence partitioning; random choice lacks the targeting that error guessing has; counting executed statements is white-box coverage.'},
 {id:'c4-018',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.1',k:'K2',q:'A tester keeps a list of past failure types (empty fields, very long names, cancelled payments) and writes tests aimed at each. Which statement best describes this?',o:['It is a structured way of applying error guessing, using a list of defects and failures.','It is statement testing, since each failure type corresponds to one statement in the code.','It is decision table testing, since there are several conditions that must be combined.','It is acceptance test-driven development, since the tests are aimed at user stories.'],a:0,why:'Correct: building a list of likely errors, defects and failures and designing tests against each is a fault attack, a way of implementing error guessing. Wrong: statement testing, decision tables and ATDD derive tests from code structure, rule combinations or story criteria instead.'},
 {id:'c4-019',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.1',k:'K2',q:'Which source of information is MOST typical for informing error guessing?',o:['A map of the code branches, showing every decision and its outcomes in each module.','Knowledge of how similar software failed and typical developer mistakes.','A state transition diagram of the system, with its states and events.','The percentage of requirements that are covered by at least one executed test case.'],a:1,why:'Correct: error guessing is driven by experience of errors, defects and failures. Wrong: branch maps are white-box inputs, state diagrams feed state transition testing, and requirements coverage is a measure rather than a source of likely errors.'},
 {id:'c4-020',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.2',k:'K2',q:'Which statement best describes session-based exploratory testing?',o:['Tests are written in full before the session and run unchanged.','Testing is done with no time limit and with no objectives set.','Tests are designed, run and evaluated within a time-boxed session guided by a test charter, followed by a debrief.','Tests are generated automatically from the branches of the code.'],a:2,why:'Correct: sessions are time-boxed, guided by a charter of test objectives and followed by a debriefing. Wrong: fully pre-written tests are scripted testing; having no limit or objectives is not session-based; branch-based generation is white-box.'},
 {id:'c4-021',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.2',k:'K2',q:'In which situation is exploratory testing especially valuable?',o:['When approved, detailed specifications exist and the schedule leaves plenty of time to follow them.','When the only goal is to measure how much of the code has been covered by tests.','When every test must be repeated identically on each run so that results can be compared.','When specifications are poor and time is short, so testers learn while designing tests.'],a:3,why:'Correct: exploratory testing is useful when specifications are few or inadequate, or there is significant time pressure. Wrong: well-specified, unhurried work suits formal techniques; coverage measurement is white-box; identical repeats suit scripted or automated tests.'},
 {id:'c4-022',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.2',k:'K2',q:'Which pairing correctly describes an element of an exploratory test session?',o:['Test charter - the test objectives that guide the session.','Timebox - the final test report sent to management.','Debriefing - the full list of expected results written beforehand.','Test charter - the list of defects the tester is required to find.'],a:0,why:'Correct: the charter contains the test objectives that guide the session. Wrong: the timebox is the fixed duration, not a report; the debriefing is a discussion of the session results afterwards; a charter states objectives, it does not predefine defects.'},
 {id:'c4-023',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.2',k:'K2',q:'How does exploratory testing differ most from scripted testing?',o:['It never uses notes, session sheets or any other record of what the tester did during testing.','Design, execution and evaluation happen together, with each result shaping the next test.','It can only be performed by the developers who built the product that is being tested.','It requires every test case to be fully written out before the session can even begin.'],a:1,why:'Correct: simultaneous design, execution, evaluation and learning is the key difference. Wrong: session sheets may be used to record steps and discoveries; testers, developers and others can do it, though experience helps; pre-written tests describe scripted testing.'},
 {id:'c4-024',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.3',k:'K2',q:'Which statement best explains checklist-based testing?',o:['Tests are derived from the branches and decisions found in the code of the product.','Tests are executed once from a fixed script with exact steps, data and expected results.','Tests cover items on a checklist built from experience and knowledge of how software fails.','Tests are generated from the columns of a decision table that covers all conditions.'],a:2,why:'Correct: the checklist is built from experience and an understanding of what matters to users and how software fails. Wrong: branches are white-box; fixed scripts are scripted testing; decision tables belong to a black-box technique.'},
 {id:'c4-025',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.3',k:'K2',q:'How should a test checklist be maintained to stay useful?',o:['Never change it, so that results stay comparable across releases and between testers.','Keep it private to its author, so that nobody changes it by accident between releases.','Replace it with a longer list each release, regardless of the defects that were found.','Update it from defect analysis: add items for new severe defects and drop stale ones.'],a:3,why:'Correct: checklists should be updated from defect analysis, while keeping them from growing too long. Wrong: a static checklist goes stale as developers learn to avoid old mistakes; secrecy has no benefit; a longer list ignores defect evidence and becomes unwieldy.'},
 {id:'c4-026',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.4.3',k:'K2',q:'A checklist item reads "Can a customer pay by card, cash and voucher?" What is a likely consequence of using high-level items like this?',o:['Testing may vary between testers: potentially greater coverage, but less repeatability.','Every tester will run exactly the same steps with exactly the same test data every time.','The checklist can only be used by automated tools and never by testers themselves.','The coverage of the item will be identical on every run, whoever happens to test it.'],a:0,why:'Correct: high-level items leave room for variation, which can widen coverage but reduces repeatability. Wrong: identical steps would need detailed scripts; checklists are used by testers directly; identical coverage on every run is not guaranteed.'},
 {id:'c4-027',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.1',k:'K2',q:'In the 3 Cs of a user story, what does Confirmation refer to?',o:['The product owner signing off the sprint.','The acceptance criteria that show the story is done.','The developer confirming the estimate.','The customer confirming payment.'],a:1,why:'Correct: Confirmation is the set of acceptance criteria. Wrong: sprint sign-off, estimates and payment are not part of the 3 Cs, which are Card, Conversation and Confirmation.'},
 {id:'c4-028',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.1',k:'K2',q:'The N in INVEST stands for Negotiable. Which statement best explains why this is a quality of a good user story?',o:['The story is priced and agreed in a fixed contract before any work on it begins.','The story is simple enough to be checked quickly by an outside negotiator.','Details are open to discussion between business and team rather than fixed in advance.','The story can be dropped from the iteration at any time without talking to anyone.'],a:2,why:'Correct: a negotiable story captures the need, and its details are worked out through collaboration. Wrong: a fixed contract is the opposite of negotiable; a negotiator is irrelevant; dropping a story without discussion is not what the term means.'},
 {id:'c4-029',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.1',k:'K2',q:'How does a tester best contribute when a user story is being written with developers and business representatives?',o:['By writing the code for the story as soon as the first draft has been written down.','By waiting until the story is finished before looking at it for the first time.','By only checking the spelling and grammar of the story text before it is approved.','By asking questions that expose ambiguity and by helping make the story testable.'],a:3,why:'Correct: the tester adds the testing perspective, spotting ambiguity and untestable statements. Wrong: coding is not the tester\'s role here; waiting loses early feedback; spelling checks alone add little value.'},
 {id:'c4-030',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.1',k:'K2',q:'Which story is LEAST well aligned with the "Testable" part of INVEST?',o:['"As a customer I want the app to feel fast and modern."','"As a customer I want to see my total before paying."','"As a barista I want unpaid orders blocked from the Ready list."','"As a manager I want to export daily sales as a CSV file."'],a:0,why:'Correct: "fast and modern" has no measurable condition that could be verified. Wrong: the other stories describe observable, verifiable behaviour.'},
 {id:'c4-031',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.2',k:'K2',q:'Acceptance criterion: "Given an unpaid order, when the barista taps Ready, then the till shows payment required." How is it classified?',o:['Rule-oriented','Scenario-oriented','Checklist-oriented','Coverage-oriented'],a:1,why:'Correct: Given/When/Then is the scenario-oriented format. Wrong: rule-oriented uses rule lists or input-output tables; checklist-oriented and coverage-oriented are not acceptance criteria formats in the syllabus.'},
 {id:'c4-032',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.2',k:'K2',q:'Acceptance criteria are written as: "Orders of R200 or more deliver free. Orders below R200 pay R25. Delivery is only within 10 km." How are they classified?',o:['Scenario-oriented','Rule-oriented','State-oriented','Exploratory'],a:1,why:'Correct: a list of rules to verify is rule-oriented. Wrong: there is no Given/When/Then scenario; state-oriented and exploratory are not acceptance criteria formats.'},
 {id:'c4-033',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.2',k:'K2',q:'A table maps order total to delivery fee: under R200 gives R25, R200 and over gives R0. How is this format classified?',o:['Scenario-oriented','Not allowed as acceptance criteria','Rule-oriented','Experience-based'],a:2,why:'Correct: a tabulated input-output mapping is a rule-oriented format. Wrong: it has no Given/When/Then; tables are a valid format; experience-based is a family of test techniques, not a criteria format.'},
 {id:'c4-034',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.2',k:'K2',q:'Which is a purpose of acceptance criteria?',o:['To replace all later testing of the story once the criteria are signed off.','To state the exact code the developer must write to satisfy the story.','To record the defects that were found in production after release.','To define the story\'s scope and give a basis for acceptance tests stakeholders agree on.'],a:3,why:'Correct: criteria define scope, build consensus and underpin acceptance testing. Wrong: they do not replace testing, do not prescribe code, and do not record defects.'},
 {id:'c4-035',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.3',k:'K3',q:'Story: "As a customer I want free delivery on bigger orders." Criteria: orders of R200 or more deliver free; orders below R200 pay a R25 fee. Which acceptance test is consistent with the criteria and checks the non-free case?',o:['Order total R250; expect a delivery fee of R25.','Order total R180; expect free delivery.','Order total R200; expect a delivery fee of R25.','Order total R180; expect a delivery fee of R25.'],a:3,why:'Correct: R180 is below R200, so the fee is R25. Wrong: R250 should be free; R180 free contradicts the criteria; R200 should be free because the threshold is "or more".'},
 {id:'c4-036',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.3',k:'K3',q:'Story: "As a registered customer I want to reset my password by email." Criteria: (a) a reset link is sent to the registered email; (b) the link expires after 30 minutes; (c) an unregistered email address shows a neutral message and sends nothing. Which set of acceptance tests covers all criteria with correct expectations?',o:['Registered email gets a link and a link used after 10 minutes works.','Unregistered email gets a link; a link used after 30 minutes works.','Registered email gets a link; a link used after 31 minutes is rejected; an unregistered email shows a neutral message and no email is sent.','Registered email gets no link; unregistered email gets a link.'],a:2,why:'Correct: it covers criteria a, b and c with expectations that match them. Wrong: the set with only a 10-minute link never tests expiry or the unregistered case; the set where an unregistered address gets a link contradicts the neutral-message rule; the set where the registered email gets no link reverses the expected behaviour.'},
 {id:'c4-037',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.3',k:'K3',q:'Criterion: "A barista cannot mark an order Ready until payment is confirmed." Which Given/When/Then is a well-formed acceptance test for it?',o:['Given an unpaid order, when the barista tries to mark it Ready, then the system refuses and shows that payment is pending.','Given a paid order, when the barista marks it Ready, then the system refuses.','Given an unpaid order, when the barista marks it Ready, then the order becomes Ready.','Given the system, when it is used, then it should work well.'],a:0,why:'Correct: it has a precondition, an action and an observable expected result matching the criterion. Wrong: refusing a paid order contradicts the intent; making an unpaid order Ready breaks the criterion; the "work well" test is vague and untestable.'},
 {id:'c4-038',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.3',k:'K3',q:'Story: "As a manager I want an alert when a stock item falls below 5 units." In ATDD, which is the best FIRST acceptance test to write?',o:['Stock of exactly 5 units; expect that no alert is shown to the manager.','Stock of -1 units entered by mistake; expect an error message to appear.','10 000 stock updates arrive in one minute; expect every alert within 1 second.','An item drops from 8 to 3 units; expect an alert shown to the manager.'],a:3,why:'Correct: the first test should be the main positive scenario, with no exceptions or error conditions. Wrong: the 5-unit case is a boundary follow-up; invalid stock values are a negative test; heavy-load alerting is a later non-functional test.'},
 {id:'c4-039',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.3',k:'K3',q:'Story: loyalty stamps. Criteria: each paid coffee adds 1 stamp; when the card shows 10 stamps the next coffee is free and the card resets to 0; a free coffee adds no stamp. Which set of acceptance tests matches the criteria?',o:['A card at 9 plus a paid coffee shows 10 stamps; with 10 stamps the next coffee costs R0 and the card resets to 0; after that free coffee the card still shows 0.','A card at 9 plus a paid coffee makes that coffee free; the card shows 0.','A card at 10 gives a free next coffee and then shows 1 stamp.','A card at 10 is charged normally for the next coffee and then shows 11.'],a:0,why:'Correct: the three tests follow all three criteria. Wrong: making the coffee that earns the 10th stamp free is wrong, because the free coffee is the NEXT one; showing 1 stamp after a free coffee contradicts the rule that a free coffee adds no stamp; charging normally and showing 11 ignores both the free coffee and the reset.'},
 {id:'c4-040',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.5.3',k:'K3',q:'Story: "As a customer I want to limit my order size." Criteria: an order may contain up to 5 coffees; trying to add a 6th shows the message "Maximum 5 per order" and the order stays at 5. Which acceptance test checks the negative scenario?',o:['Add 5 coffees one after another; expect all five to be accepted without any message.','With 5 coffees, add a 6th; expect the message "Maximum 5 per order" and 5 coffees to remain.','Add 1 coffee; expect the price of that coffee to be shown on the order screen.','With 5 coffees, add a 6th; expect it to be accepted and the order to hold 6 coffees.'],a:1,why:'Correct: it tests the limit being enforced, with the specified message and result. Wrong: adding 5 is the positive case; showing a price is not in the criteria; accepting a 6th contradicts them.'},
 {id:'c4-041',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.1',k:'K3',q:'A ticket price depends on age. Valid ages are 0 to 120 (whole numbers only; ignore fractional and non-numeric input): child 0-12, adult 13-64, senior 65-120. Anything else is rejected. What is the minimum number of tests needed for 100% equivalence partitioning coverage, including invalid partitions?',o:['3','5','4','7'],a:1,why:'Correct: the partitions are below 0 (invalid), 0-12, 13-64, 65-120 and above 120 (invalid): 5 partitions, and one test per partition is enough. Wrong: 3 forgets the invalid partitions; 4 forgets one of the two invalid partitions; 7 tests several values from the same partition, which adds no coverage.'},
 {id:'c4-042',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.1',k:'K3',q:'Same ticket rule: valid ages 0 to 120 (whole numbers only), child 0-12, adult 13-64, senior 65-120, other values rejected. Which set of test inputs achieves 100% equivalence partitioning coverage?',o:['-3, 8, 30, 70, 130','8, 30, 70','5, 8, 12, 13, 30','-3, 8, 30, 70'],a:0,why:'Correct: -3 (invalid low), 8 (child), 30 (adult), 70 (senior) and 130 (invalid high) hit all 5 partitions. Wrong: 8, 30, 70 skips both invalid partitions; 5, 8, 12, 13, 30 takes three values from the child partition and misses senior and both invalid ones; -3, 8, 30, 70 misses the invalid high partition.'},
 {id:'c4-043',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.1',k:'K3',q:'A checkout form has two inputs: payment type (card, cash, voucher; 3 valid partitions) and delivery mode (pickup, delivery; 2 valid partitions). Ignoring invalid values, what is the minimum number of tests for 100% Each Choice coverage?',o:['2','5','6','3'],a:3,why:'Correct: each test uses one payment type and one delivery mode, so 3 tests can cover all 3 payment partitions while also covering both delivery partitions. Wrong: 2 cannot cover 3 payment types; 5 adds the partitions as if each test covered only one; 6 is the number of combinations, which Each Choice coverage does not require.'},
 {id:'c4-044',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.2',k:'K3',q:'An order may contain 1 to 12 cups (whole numbers). Which set of test values gives 100% coverage of 2-value boundary value analysis?',o:['1, 12','0, 1, 12, 13','0, 1, 2, 11, 12, 13','1, 2, 11, 12'],a:1,why:'Correct: boundary values are 1 and 12 (the min and max of the valid partition); with 2-value BVA each is paired with its closest neighbour in the adjacent partition: 0 and 13. Wrong: 1 and 12 alone leave out the neighbours; 0, 1, 2, 11, 12, 13 is the 3-value set; 1, 2, 11, 12 takes neighbours inside the valid partition instead of the adjacent partitions.'},
 {id:'c4-045',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.2',k:'K3',q:'An order total must be R20 to R500 inclusive (whole rands) to qualify for delivery. Which set is the complete set of 3-value boundary value analysis test values for the two boundaries of the valid partition?',o:['19, 20, 500, 501','20, 21, 499, 500','19, 20, 21, 499, 500, 501','20, 100, 500'],a:2,why:'Correct: for the boundary 20 the values are 19, 20, 21; for the boundary 500 they are 499, 500, 501: 6 values in total. Wrong: 19, 20, 500, 501 is the 2-value set; 20, 21, 499, 500 stays inside the valid partition and ignores the neighbours outside; 20, 100, 500 uses a middle value that is not a boundary or neighbour.'},
 {id:'c4-046',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.2',k:'K3',q:'Requirement: customers ordering 10 cups or fewer get a free sample ("cups <= 10"). The code wrongly says "cups = 10". Looking at the boundary between 10 and 11, which statement is correct?',o:['2-value BVA (10 and 11) is sure to detect this defect; 3-value BVA adds nothing.','Neither 2-value nor 3-value BVA could detect this defect.','The test value 9, which comes from 3-value BVA, would reveal the defect; 10 and 11 alone would not.','Only the value 11 can reveal the defect, because it lies in the invalid partition.'],a:2,why:'Correct: at 10 and 11 the wrong code and the right code behave the same (sample at 10, none at 11); at 9 the right code gives a sample but the wrong code does not, so 9 exposes the defect. Wrong: 2-value BVA misses it; 3-value BVA detects it; 11 behaves identically in both versions.'},
 {id:'c4-047',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.2',k:'K3',q:'An order may contain 1 to 12 cups. A tester runs 2-value BVA tests for 0, 1 and 12 only. What is the 2-value BVA coverage?',o:['100%','75%','50%','60%'],a:1,why:'Correct: 2-value BVA identifies 4 coverage items (0, 1, 12 and 13); 3 were exercised: 3 of 4 = 75%. Wrong: 100% would need 13 as well; 50% and 60% undercount.'},
 {id:'c4-048',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.3',k:'K3',q:'A delivery rule depends on three yes/no conditions: customer is a member, order is R200 or more, payment is by card. How many columns does the FULL decision table have before any simplification?',o:['6','9','3','8'],a:3,why:'Correct: 3 yes/no conditions give 2 x 2 x 2 = 8 combinations. Wrong: 6 comes from adding instead of multiplying (2+2+2); 9 comes from 3 x 3; 3 is just the number of conditions.'},
 {id:'c4-049',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.3',k:'K3',q:'Rules: (1) member and total of R100 or more: 15% discount and free delivery; (2) member and total below R100: 15% discount only; (3) non-member and total of R100 or more: free delivery only; (4) non-member and total below R100: nothing. A non-member orders R150. What should happen?',o:['A 15% discount and free delivery.','A 15% discount only.','Free delivery only.','Nothing.'],a:2,why:'Correct: non-member with R100 or more matches rule 3 of the table, which gives free delivery only. Wrong: a discount and free delivery is rule 1 (members); a discount only is rule 2 (members under R100); nothing is rule 4 (under R100).'},
 {id:'c4-050',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.3',k:'K3',q:'After removing infeasible combinations and merging columns, a decision table has 3 feasible columns. Tests exercise 2 of them. What is the decision table coverage?',o:['50%','75%','67%','33%'],a:2,why:'Correct: coverage = exercised columns / feasible columns = 2 / 3, about 67%. Wrong: 50% and 75% use the wrong totals; 33% is the share not yet tested (1 of 3).'},
 {id:'c4-051',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.4',k:'K3',q:'Order states: Placed, Paid, Ready, Collected, Cancelled. Valid transitions: Placed-pay->Paid, Paid-markReady->Ready, Ready-collect->Collected, Placed-cancel->Cancelled, Paid-cancel->Cancelled. Collected and Cancelled are final (no way out). What is the minimum number of tests for 100% valid transitions coverage?',o:['2','4','5','3'],a:3,why:'Correct: test 1 pay, markReady, collect (3 transitions); test 2 cancel from Placed; test 3 pay, then cancel from Paid. Both cancel paths end in a final state, so they cannot be merged: 3 tests. Wrong: 2 cannot cover both cancel paths and the pay-ready-collect chain; 4 and 5 assume one test per transition or extra tests that are not needed.'},
 {id:'c4-052',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.4',k:'K3',q:'Same order model: Placed, Paid, Ready, Collected, Cancelled with valid transitions Placed-pay->Paid, Paid-markReady->Ready, Ready-collect->Collected, Placed-cancel->Cancelled, Paid-cancel->Cancelled. What is the minimum number of tests for 100% all states coverage?',o:['1','3','5','2'],a:3,why:'Correct: pay, markReady, collect visits Placed, Paid, Ready and Collected; a second test cancelling from Placed visits Cancelled: 2 tests. Wrong: 1 test cannot end in both Collected and Cancelled; 3 or 5 are more than needed because one test can visit several states.'},
 {id:'c4-053',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.4',k:'K3',q:'Same order model. For all transitions coverage, which test attempts an INVALID transition?',o:['Place an order, pay it, then mark it Ready; expect the state to become Ready.','Place an order, then cancel it straight away; expect the state to become Cancelled.','Place an order, pay, then cancel the order; expect the state to become Cancelled.','Place an order, mark it Ready unpaid; expect a refusal and the state to stay Placed.'],a:3,why:'Correct: markReady is not allowed from Placed, so attempting it tests an invalid transition (one invalid transition per test avoids defect masking). Wrong: the other three tests use only valid transitions from the model.'},
 {id:'c4-054',ch:4,lo:'FL-4.2.4',k:'K3',q:'Same order model (5 states, 5 valid transitions). Only one test is run: place, pay, markReady, collect. What are the valid transitions coverage and the all states coverage?',o:['Valid transitions 60%, all states 80%','Valid transitions 80%, all states 60%','Valid transitions 100%, all states 60%','Valid transitions 60%, all states 100%'],a:0,why:'Correct: the test takes 3 of 5 valid transitions = 60%, and visits Placed, Paid, Ready and Collected = 4 of 5 states = 80% (Cancelled is never visited). Wrong: the other options swap the two figures or claim 100% for something that is not complete.'}
);
EXAMDATA.terms.push(
 {t:'Black-box test technique',ch:4,d:'A way of designing tests from the expected behaviour in the requirements or other specifications, without looking at the code inside.'},
 {t:'White-box test technique',ch:4,d:'A way of designing tests from the internal structure of the software, such as its code, and measuring how much of that structure the tests touched.'},
 {t:'Experience-based test technique',ch:4,d:'A way of designing tests using the knowledge and skill of people, such as testers, developers and users, rather than a formal model.'},
 {t:'Equivalence partitioning',ch:4,d:'Dividing inputs or outputs into groups that should behave the same way, then testing one value from each group.'},
 {t:'Boundary value analysis',ch:4,d:'Testing the values at the edges of ordered partitions, where mistakes are most likely; can use 2 or 3 values per boundary.'},
 {t:'Decision table testing',ch:4,d:'Listing combinations of conditions and their resulting actions in a table, then testing each rule (column).'},
 {t:'State transition testing',ch:4,d:'Designing tests from a model of states and the events that move the software from one state to another.'},
 {t:'Statement coverage',ch:4,d:'The percentage of executable statements in the code that the tests ran at least once.'},
 {t:'Branch coverage',ch:4,d:'The percentage of branches, such as the true and false outcomes of decisions, that the tests took; stronger than statement coverage.'},
 {t:'Coverage',ch:4,d:'How much of a chosen item, such as code, requirements or partitions, has been exercised by the tests, shown as a percentage.'},
 {t:'Error guessing',ch:4,d:'Designing tests to hit mistakes you expect, based on experience of past errors, defects and failures.'},
 {t:'Exploratory testing',ch:4,d:'Testing where you design, run and learn from tests at the same time, often in time-boxed sessions with a charter.'},
 {t:'Checklist-based testing',ch:4,d:'Testing that covers the items on a checklist built from experience and knowledge of how software fails.'},
 {t:'Test charter',ch:4,d:'A short statement of the objectives for an exploratory testing session.'},
 {t:'User story',ch:4,d:'A short description of a feature from the point of view of a user, saying who wants what and why.'},
 {t:'Acceptance criteria',ch:4,d:'The conditions a feature must meet to be accepted by the customer or other stakeholders.'},
 {t:'Acceptance test-driven development (ATDD)',ch:4,d:'A test-first approach where acceptance tests are agreed from user stories and criteria before the code is written.'},
 {t:'Test condition',ch:4,d:'An item or event of a component or system that could be verified by one or more test cases, such as a function or a boundary.'}
);
